  ('b98cf8f6-d75a-43ae-93c4-da5ec6961664','Split Nock, based in Waseca in south-central Minnesota, runs a Junior Olympic Archery Development program under USA Archery affiliation, giving young archers in this farm-country region a structured, recognized pathway into the sport. Waseca sits roughly halfway between Mankato and Owatonna, and a JOAD program here serves families across a fairly wide stretch of rural Minnesota who might not otherwise have easy access to organized youth archery instruction. No public details on lane counts, indoor or outdoor facility type, or hours are listed for Split Nock. Minnesota''s long winters typically push programs like this indoors for part of the year. Contact Split Nock directly for session schedules.'),
  ('bee43279-c648-45bc-9ef5-ac3a36e8bc06','Split Nocks Archers, based in Powhatan west of Richmond, holds ASA affiliation linking it to Virginia''s 3D bowhunting archery community, a name that echoes but is otherwise unrelated to the similarly named Split Nock JOAD program up in Minnesota. Piedmont Virginia''s mix of rolling farmland and hardwood forest around Powhatan makes solid terrain for a 3D animal-target course, giving local hunters realistic practice ahead of Virginia''s deer season. As a volunteer-run club, operations depend on member time and dues rather than paid staff, and public listings don''t include lane counts or course length. Local bowhunters interested in Split Nocks Archers should contact the club directly for shoot schedules.'),
  ('2ae78ab7-f1bf-4b68-bf32-173a45efd294','Spokane JOAD carries its program right in the name: a USA Archery affiliated club in Spokane, Washington built around the Junior Olympic Archery Development pathway that feeds young archers toward regional and national competition. Lessons are part of what''s offered here, since new JOAD archers typically start with basic form and safety instruction before progressing to scored rounds. Eastern Washington has a growing youth archery scene, and a dedicated JOAD affiliate gives local families a structured, coached alternative to informal backyard shooting. Practice schedules, fees, and age groups aren''t listed publicly, so parents interested in enrolling a young archer should contact the club directly for current details.'),
  ('956eaf88-1e6f-4b14-97f7-20c472c8967e','Spot on Archery sits in Fife Lake, a small northern Michigan community, and holds USA Archery affiliation with both a Junior Olympic Archery Development track and an Adult Archery Program on its roster. Running a youth pathway and an adult-focused offering under one roof is efficient for a small-town club, letting one facility serve families across age groups rather than splitting into separate youth-only and adult-only operations. Northern Michigan''s archery scene tends to be spread across small clubs like this rather than concentrated in big commercial operations, so a dual-program affiliate stands out locally. Meeting times and how to join aren''t listed, so reach out directly for specifics.'),
  ('6aa34313-f629-425c-ba96-1f1d0043a895','Square1Outdoors, Inc. Youth Archery Initiative, based in Tupelo, Mississippi, is USA Archery affiliated and runs an unusually broad set of programs: Junior Olympic Archery Development, an Adult Archery Program, 3D Archery, and Olympic Archery in the Schools. That last program signals a partnership reaching into local schools to introduce archery as part of physical education or after-school activity, a step beyond what most club-level affiliates take on. Tupelo sits in northeast Mississippi, an area with a solid hunting and target archery following, and a group offering this many pathways likely serves as a hub for archers of very different ages. Enrollment details for each program aren''t listed, so contact the organization directly.'),
  ('9ad39b77-2ec0-48e4-8ee4-d6ef5076c527','In Ashville, Alabama, St. Clair Archery Club runs as a volunteer club with an ASA affiliation, placing it in the 3D archery and bowhunting-style circuit that ASA clubs across the South typically follow. Jeff Boland is listed as the club''s contact, a practical starting point for anyone wanting shoot dates, course layout, or membership details, since day-to-day operations here aren''t posted publicly. St. Clair County sits in the foothills north of Birmingham, an area with a strong hunting tradition, and a 3D-format club fits naturally into that landscape by giving archers realistic-target practice ahead of deer season. Contact the club for anything more specific.'),
  ('b51a3c6d-de0c-4ae8-81fd-f96835aaa5ff','St. Louis Archery Club is a USA Archery affiliated hybrid operation running both a Junior Olympic Archery Development program and an Adult Archery Program, with lessons offered as part of the mix. For a major metro area like St. Louis, a club that teaches beginners of any age, rather than only serving archers who already own equipment and know the fundamentals, is a genuine on-ramp for newcomers. The JOAD and adult tracks running side by side suggest a facility set up for family participation rather than a single narrow age band. Missouri has a deep archery and bowhunting culture, making this a reasonable first call. Contact the club directly for class times and costs.'),
  ('27fb8c67-8aba-462b-a376-e2f4b3de5cfb','Stand Outdoors operates in Ulman, a small community in the Lake of the Ozarks region of Missouri, as a volunteer club with an ASA affiliation putting it on the 3D archery and bowhunting circuit. Wesley Smith is the listed contact, the right person to reach for shoot schedules, course conditions, or membership questions, since the club doesn''t appear to publish much of that information elsewhere. The Ozarks region draws heavy hunting traffic each fall, and a 3D-format club here likely gives local bowhunters a chance to sharpen range estimation before season opens. No lane counts or amenities are listed, so Wesley Smith is the best source for specifics.'),
  ('78c06752-01ef-4965-95a6-100debb3d471','Star Archery, located in Pulaski, Tennessee, appears in ASA''s records as an affiliated archery club, though the listed status shows the affiliation as expired as of 2025. Anyone relying on this listing should treat the ASA connection as historical rather than a guarantee of current standing, and should confirm directly with the club whether it''s still active. Pulaski sits in south-central Tennessee, an area with an active hunting and 3D archery scene, so a club here would fit that regional pattern if it''s still running. Without current program details or membership information on file, the honest next step is a direct call to verify status before making plans.'),
  ('87131988-c054-43cf-9b68-86d4d03dbfb6','Smithton, Missouri is a small town roughly midway between Sedalia and Jefferson City, and it''s where State Fair Archery Club runs as a volunteer, ASA-affiliated 3D archery club. The name likely nods to the nearby Missouri State Fairgrounds in Sedalia, though the club itself operates independently as a member-run outfit rather than a fairgrounds attraction. Kelly Dore is listed as the contact, a good first call for questions about shoot dates or how to join, since the club''s schedule isn''t published here. Central Missouri has a steady base of 3D and bowhunting-style archery, and clubs like this typically serve as a low-key local gathering spot.'),
  ('d3d94412-61cd-4a89-b3b8-13d40cc95980','Staten Island Archery Edge shows up in the IBO directory as an archery pro shop serving the Staten Island area of New York City, a borough where dedicated archery retail is relatively rare compared to its suburban counterparts. Being listed with the International Bowhunting Organization suggests a business oriented at least partly toward bowhunters and 3D-style shooters rather than purely Olympic recurve target archery. Staten Island''s mix of dense residential neighborhoods and pockets of parkland makes indoor or retail-based archery access more valuable than it might be in a more rural setting. No details on lanes, lessons, or hours are listed, so contact the business directly to confirm what''s currently available.'),
  ('bfeca7fa-345e-4086-b53c-e36e55d94019','Hurt, Virginia, a small town along the Staunton River in the south-central part of the state, is where Staunton River Archery Club runs as a volunteer, ASA-affiliated 3D archery club. David Simpson is the listed contact for the club, the person to reach for questions about shoot dates or joining, since the operation doesn''t appear to publish a regular public schedule. The river-adjacent, rural setting fits the kind of terrain ASA-style 3D courses typically use, with wooded and open ground for realistic target placement. Southern Virginia has a strong bowhunting tradition, and a 3D club here likely serves as a seasonal tune-up spot for hunters. Contact David Simpson for current information.'),
  ('aec38627-70a7-4481-b010-13fbfe9890a3','Stockbridge Sportsmen Buckhorn operates in Stockbridge, a small town in the Berkshires of western Massachusetts, as a volunteer club carrying an ASA 3D archery affiliation. John Mange is listed as the contact, the person to reach for questions on shoot dates or how membership works, since the club''s schedule isn''t posted publicly here. The Berkshires'' hilly, wooded terrain is well suited to a 3D-style course, and clubs in this part of Massachusetts often double as a hub for both target archers and local bowhunters preparing for the fall season. There''s no listed detail on lane count beyond the ASA affiliation itself, so John Mange is the right contact for specifics before visiting.')
) AS v(id, description)
WHERE r.id = v.id::uuid AND r.is_claimed = false;
